//! # Zamani Universal Runtime — Code Integrity
//!
//! Path: src/zur/security/code_integrity.rs
//! Edition: Rust 2021
//! Minimum Rust version: 1.97
//! Safety: Safe Rust only.
//!
//! # Purpose
//!
//! Provides the ZUR security boundary for verifying the integrity and
//! authenticity of compiler-produced artifacts and generated executable
//! code before they are accepted for execution.
//!
//! # Ownership
//!
//! This module owns:
//!
//! - Runtime artifact-integrity verification.
//! - Expected-digest comparison.
//! - Cryptographic artifact-signature verification.
//! - Trust-authority integration.
//! - Provenance structural validation.
//! - Artifact identity and metadata binding.
//! - Immutable verification receipts.
//! - Fail-closed integrity policy enforcement.
//!
//! This module does NOT own:
//!
//! - Artifact construction.
//! - Artifact payload storage.
//! - Artifact manifest construction.
//! - Compiler provenance generation.
//! - Cryptographic key generation.
//! - Private-key storage.
//! - Trust-store persistence.
//! - Authentication.
//! - Authorization.
//! - Semantic verification.
//! - IR verification.
//! - Backend selection.
//! - Code generation.
//! - Executable-memory management.
//! - Hardware discovery.
//! - Resource reservation.
//! - Scheduling.
//! - Execution.
//!
//! These responsibilities remain with their existing authorities.
//!
//! # Canonical integration
//!
//! Artifact:
//!     crate::backend::artifact::OutputArtifact
//!
//! Artifact payload:
//!     crate::backend::artifact::ArtifactPayload
//!
//! Artifact manifest:
//!     crate::backend::manifest
//!
//! Runtime error:
//!     crate::zur::error
//!
//! JIT:
//!     crate::zur::jit::verification
//!
//! Runtime authorization:
//!     crate::zur::security::permissions
//!
//! Runtime isolation:
//!     crate::zur::security::isolation
//!
//! # Security invariants
//!
//! 1. An artifact must have a trusted expected digest.
//! 2. The digest is calculated from the exact artifact payload bytes.
//! 3. An artifact's self-reported digest is not independently trusted.
//! 4. A digest is not a digital signature.
//! 5. A valid signature is not proof of semantic correctness.
//! 6. A signature must be checked against a trusted public key.
//! 7. Unknown or revoked signing keys must not authorize an artifact.
//! 8. Signature verification must bind the exact artifact identity,
//!    target, format, payload size, digest and provenance.
//! 9. Provenance metadata is not authenticated merely because it exists.
//! 10. Missing required evidence causes rejection.
//! 11. Verification failures must never become successful receipts.
//! 12. A verification receipt is not an execution result.
//! 13. Successful verification does not reserve resources.
//! 14. Verification must happen before executable code is accepted.
//! 15. No global mutable trust state is maintained.
//!
//! # Scalability
//!
//! No arbitrary maximum is imposed on:
//!
//! - Artifact sizes.
//! - Artifact counts.
//! - Computational domains.
//! - Providers.
//! - Signing identities.
//! - Provenance transformations.
//! - Artifact dependencies.
//! - Hardware resources.
//!
//! Actual memory and processing requirements remain subject to
//! available resources and platform constraints.
//!
//! # POCO-REAF
//!
//! Integrity verification is independent of the physical execution
//! target. The same verification contract applies to compatible
//! classical, quantum, HDL, AI, accelerator, hybrid, distributed,
//! photonic and future-domain artifacts.
//!
//! This module does not claim that an artifact is executable on
//! every target merely because its integrity is verified.

